\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Set 1 - The Real Business Cycle
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The primary paper is King and Rebelo (2000).
 This set of lecture notes presents a basic overview of the model, as well
 as providing an alternative solution method to Dyanmic Programming, known
 as the Perturbation Method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model is established by combining several components 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Preferences:}\ E_{0}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(C_{t},L_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\beta<1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a utility funciton for a representative agent, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 is consumption at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset

 is leisure at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Endowments:}\ N_{t}+L_{t}=1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 is work at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Technology:}\ Y_{t}=A_{t}F(K_{t},N_{t}X_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}$
\end_inset

 is output at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a productivity shock at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a production function with constant returns to scale, 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{t}$
\end_inset

 is capital at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the deterministic component of productivity where we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t+1}=\gamma X_{t}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma>1$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 is known as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

labor augmenting growth.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Resource Constraint:}\ Y_{t}=C_{t}+I_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{t}$
\end_inset

 is investment at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Capital Accumulation Rule:}\ K_{t+1}=I_{t}+(1-\delta)K_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 is the depreciation rate of capital
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Initial Conditions:}\ K_{0}>0,X_{0}>0,A_{0}>0\ \mbox{are given}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can frame the RBC model into mathematical form, and we determine its
 solution by solving the following constrained maximization problem:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(C_{t},L_{t})\right]\\
\mbox{s.t.} & N_{t}+L_{t}=1\\
 & Y_{t}=A_{t}K_{t}^{1-\alpha}(N_{t}X_{t})^{\alpha}\\
 & X_{t+1}=\gamma X_{t}\\
 & Y_{t}=C_{t}+I_{t}\\
 & K_{t+1}=I_{t}+(1-\delta)K_{t}\\
 & K_{0}>0,X_{0}>0,A_{0}>0\ \mbox{are given}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Questions to Consider
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
1.
 Where are the factor markets?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can introduce them if we want, but such modification does not change
 predictions about the endogenous variables, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $(C_{t},I_{t},N_{t},\ldots)$
\end_inset

.
 This is a consequence of the Fundamental Welfare Theorems of Economics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
2.
 Why is there a representative agent, as opposed to a continuum of consumers?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this model, we are assuming markets are complete.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Summarizing the Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We wish to maximize expected utility subject to the production technology
 and feasibility constraints of the economy.
 We can use dynamic programming to solve this problem.
 However, this method can take some time due to value function iteration
 (VFI).
 Consequently, we may need to apply other techniques to find a solution.
 In this class, we will learn how to solve the model using local methods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we setup the Lagrangian and derive the first-order conditions.
 An economic interpretation of each first-order condition has been provided.
 The Lagrangian is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathscr{L}=E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left\{ u(C_{t},1-N_{t})+\lambda_{t}(-C_{t}-K_{t+1}+(1-\delta)K_{t}+A_{t}K_{t}^{1-\alpha}(N_{t}X_{t})^{\alpha})\right\} \right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the first order conditions are listed below.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(C_{t}):\ u_{c}(t)-\lambda_{t}=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 known as the shadow price.
 It measures the marginal utility of consumption at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, i.e., the approximate additional utility obtained from an extra unit of
 consumption.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(N_{t}):\ u_{L}(t)(-1)+\lambda_{t}\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $-u_{L}(t)$
\end_inset

 is the marginal disutility of labor, i.e., it is the opportunity cost of
 losing a unit of leisure.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the approximate additional utility obtained from an extra unit of consumptio
n, while 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}$
\end_inset

 is the marginal product of labor (MPL).
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

 is the marginal utility product of labor, and it is approximately how much
 utility the agent gains when he works an extra hour.
 Thus, the marginal cost of labor equals the marginal benefit of labor.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(K_{t+1}):\ \lambda_{t}(-1)+E_{t}\left[\beta\lambda_{t+1}\left((1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t+1}}{K_{t+1}}+(1-\delta)\right)\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(-\lambda_{t})$
\end_inset

 is the approximate reduction in utility if one unit of consumption is taken
 away for investment, i.e., the marginal cost of investment.
 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t+1}}{K_{t+1}}$
\end_inset

 is the marginal product of capital (MPK).
 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\delta)$
\end_inset

 tells us how much capital is left over after production.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 is the approximate additional utility obtained from an extra unit of consumptio
n in time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

.
 Because the expression on the right side of the equation takes place in
 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, we must multiply it by the discount factor, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Also, we take expectation of the right side because what happens in 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 is random.
 So, 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}\left[\beta\lambda_{t+1}\left((1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t+1}}{K_{t+1}}+(1-\delta)\right)\right]$
\end_inset

 can be thought of as the discounted expected marginal benefit of investment,
 measured in utility.
 Thus, the marginal cost of investment equals the discounted expected marginal
 benefit of investment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using the feasibility constraints and the three first-order conditions,
 we can summarize the model as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{L}(t) & = & u_{C}(t)\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}\\
1 & = & E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{u_{C}(t+1)}{u_{C}(t)}\left((1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t+1}}{K_{t+1}}+(1-\delta)\right)\right]\\
C_{t}+I_{t} & = & Y_{t}\\
K_{t+1} & = & (1-\delta)K_{t}+I_{t}\\
Y_{t} & = & A_{t}K_{t}^{1-\alpha}(N_{t}X_{t})^{\alpha}\\
1 & = & N_{t}+L_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, we have summarized the model into a concise system of 6 equations
 and 6 endogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\{Y_{t},N_{t},L_{t},K_{t+1},C_{t},I_{t}\}$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 are assumed to be exogenous.
 Some of the difficulties that can arise from this reduced version are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Some equations are nonlinear
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Variables are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dated
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}=[\cdot]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There is a trend term, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Detrending the Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Steady State Growth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here are two important facts to consider regarding the endogenous variables
 in the real world:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t},C_{t},I_{t},K_{t}$
\end_inset

 grow, more or less, at a constant rate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 is, more or less, constant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Question: Does out model economy exhibit such a pattern?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A way to check this is to divide all the trending variables by 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 and see if we get a new system without 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we will detrend the necessary endogenous variables, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{Y_{t},C_{t},I_{t},K_{t}\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Production Functio
\series default
n
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
Y_{t} & = & A_{t}K_{t}^{1-\alpha}(N_{t}X_{t})^{\alpha}\nonumber \\
X_{t}\left(\frac{Y_{t}}{X_{t}}\right) & = & =A_{t}\left(\frac{K_{t}}{X_{t}}\right)^{1-\alpha}X_{t}^{1-\alpha}\left(N_{t}X_{t}\right)^{\alpha}\nonumber \\
X_{t}y_{t} & = & A_{t}k_{t}^{1-\alpha}X_{t}^{1-\alpha}(N_{t}X_{t})^{\alpha}\nonumber \\
y_{t} & = & A_{t}k_{t}^{1-\alpha}N_{t}^{\alpha}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\series medium
where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=\frac{Y_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\family roman
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}=\frac{K_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Capital Accumulation Rule
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
K_{t+1} & = & (1-\delta)K_{t}+I_{t}\nonumber \\
X_{t+1}\frac{K_{t+1}}{X_{t+1}} & = & (1-\delta)\frac{K_{t}}{X_{t}}X_{t}+\frac{I_{t}}{X_{t}}X_{t}\nonumber \\
X_{t+1}k_{t+1} & = & (1-\delta)k_{t}X_{t}+i_{t}X_{t}\nonumber \\
\frac{X_{t+1}}{X_{t}}k_{t+1} & = & (1-\delta)k_{t}+i_{t}\nonumber \\
\gamma k_{t+1} & = & (1-\delta)k_{t}+i_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=\frac{X_{t+1}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}=\frac{K_{t+1}}{X_{t+1}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}=\frac{K_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}=\frac{I_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Resource Constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
C_{t}+I_{t} & = & Y_{t}\nonumber \\
X_{t}\frac{C_{t}}{X_{t}}+X_{t}\frac{I_{t}}{X_{t}} & = & X_{t}\frac{Y_{t}}{X_{t}}\nonumber \\
X_{t}(c_{t}+i_{t}) & = & X_{t}y_{t}\nonumber \\
c_{t}+i_{t} & = & y_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=\frac{C_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}=\frac{I_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=\frac{Y_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Equations Involving Utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{L}(t) & = & u_{C}(t)\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}\\
1 & = & E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{u_{C}(t+1)}{u_{C}(t)}\left((1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t+1}}{K_{t+1}}+1-\delta\right)\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We need restrictions on 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 One particularly important class: King-Plasser-Rebelo (KPR) utility function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u(C,L)=\begin{cases}
\frac{1}{1-\sigma}\left(\left[C\nu(L)\right]^{1-\sigma}-1\right) & \mbox{if}\ \sigma\neq1\\
\ln C+\nu(L) & \mbox{if}\ \sigma=1
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is an increasing and concave function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 represents the relative risk aversion of the agent, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{R}^{u}(C)=-C\frac{u^{\prime\prime}(C)}{u{}^{\prime}(C)}$
\end_inset

, which is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d\log(u^{\prime}(C))}{d\log C}$
\end_inset

.
 As 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 grows, the agent's aversion to risk escalates since the utility function
 becomes more concave.
 In addition, increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 causes a larger percentage change in marginal utility when there is a percentag
e change in consumption.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{d\ln\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)}{d\ln\left(\frac{u^{\prime}(C_{t+1})}{u^{\prime}(C_{t})}\right)}=-\frac{1}{\sigma}\iff\frac{d\ln(MU)}{d\ln C}=-\sigma
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 is also considered to be the intertemporal elasticity of substitution,
 which captures the sensitivity of the intertemporal substitution, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_{t}}$
\end_inset

, to the interest rate.
 Thus, as 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 increases, consumption becomes less responsive to the interest rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we plug the utility function into the first equation by taking the
 proper derivatives, and then we detrend the necessary variables:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left[C_{t}\nu(L_{t})\right]^{-\sigma}C_{t}\nu^{\prime}(L_{t}) & = & \left[C_{t}\nu(L_{t})\right]^{-\sigma}\nu(L_{t})\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}\nonumber \\
C_{t}\nu^{\prime}(L_{t}) & = & \nu(L_{t})\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}\nonumber \\
c_{t}\nu^{\prime}(L_{t}) & = & \nu(L_{t})\alpha\frac{y_{t}}{N_{t}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=\frac{C_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=\frac{Y_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we plug the utility function into the intertemporal condition by
 taking the proper derivatives, and then we detrend the necessary variables:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
1 & = & E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{u_{C}(t+1)}{u_{C}(t)}\left((1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t+1}}{K_{t+1}}+1-\delta\right)\right]\nonumber \\
\frac{u_{C}(t+1)}{u_{C}(t)} & = & \frac{\left[C_{t+1}\nu(L_{t+1})\right]^{-\sigma}\nu(L_{t+1})}{\left[C_{t}\nu(L_{t})\right]^{-\sigma}\nu(L_{t})}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\left(\frac{\nu(L_{t+1})}{\nu(L_{t})}\right)^{1-\sigma}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left(\frac{X_{t+1}c_{t+1}}{X_{t}c_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\left(\frac{\nu(L_{t+1})}{\nu(L_{t})}\right)^{1-\sigma}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left(\gamma\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\left(\frac{\nu(L_{t+1})}{\nu(L_{t})}\right)^{1-\sigma}\nonumber \\
1 & = & E_{t}\left[\beta\left(\gamma\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\left(\frac{\nu(L_{t+1})}{\nu(L_{t})}\right)^{1-\sigma}\left((1-\alpha)\frac{y_{t+1}}{k_{t+1}}+1-\delta\right)\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=\frac{C_{t}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t+1}=\frac{C_{t+1}}{X_{t+1}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t+1}=\frac{Y_{t+1}}{X_{t+1}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t+1}=\frac{K_{t+1}}{X_{t+1}}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=\frac{X_{t+1}}{X_{t}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Lets see what will happen if 
\begin_inset Formula $u(C_{t},L_{t})=\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}+L_{t}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\neq1$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{L}(t) & = & u_{C}(t)\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}\\
1 & = & C_{t}^{-\sigma}\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}\\
 & = & \left(X_{t}c_{t}\right)^{-\sigma}\alpha\frac{X_{t}y_{t}}{N_{t}}\\
 & = & \left(X_{t}\right)^{1-\sigma}c_{t}^{-\sigma}\alpha\frac{y_{t}}{N_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}^{1-\sigma}$
\end_inset

 does not disapper.
 Therefore, we are not able to use 
\begin_inset Formula $u(C_{t},L_{t})=\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}+L_{t}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\neq1$
\end_inset

 as our utility function since labor augmenting growth, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

, does not disappear.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Explanation for KPR Utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{u_{L}(t)}{u_{C}(t)}=\alpha\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The left hand side is the marginal rate of substitution of leisure for consumpti
on.
 It represents how much the household values leisure time in terms of consumptio
n.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The right hand side is the marginal product of labor, and it is the market
 price of work.
 It grows as technology grows, and it should grow at the same rate of GDP.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The KPR utility function makes the MRS grow at a constant rate and at the
 same rate as labor productivity.
 It implies exactly offsetting income and substitution effects of wage changes
 on labor supply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
The Detrended System
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that we have derived the first-order conditions and detrended them
 to obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c_{t}\nu^{\prime}(L_{t}) & = & \nu(L_{t})\alpha\frac{y_{t}}{N_{t}}\\
1 & = & E_{t}\left[\beta\left(\beta\gamma\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\left(\frac{\nu(L_{t+1})}{\nu(L_{t})}\right)^{1-\sigma}\left((1-\alpha)\frac{y_{t+1}}{k_{t+1}}+1-\delta\right)\right]\\
c_{t}+i_{t} & = & y_{t}\\
\gamma k_{t+1} & = & (1-\delta)k_{t}+i_{t}\\
y_{t} & = & A_{t}k_{t}^{1-\alpha}N_{t}^{\alpha}\\
1 & = & N_{t}+L_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Here, we have a system of 6 equations and 6 endogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $(L_{t},c_{t},y_{t},N_{t},k_{t},i_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 We want to know find the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $(L_{t},c_{t},y_{t},N_{t},i_{t})$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $(k_{t},A_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{t}$
\end_inset

 is an endogenous state variable.
 As stated before, we can always apply dynamic programming to solve the
 model by finding the optimal value and policy functions.
 The 6 equations above provide very useful information that we wish to utilize,
 but how, exactly? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Simplified Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us work with a simplified version of the RBC model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\ln(C_{t})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & C_{t}+K_{t+1}=K_{t}^{1-\alpha}+(1-\delta)K_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this simple model, there are no stochastic shocks, no endogenous labor
 supply, and no deterministic variable.
 The Lagrangian of this constrained maximizaiton problem is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left[\ln(C_{t})+\lambda_{t}\left(-C_{t}-K_{t+1}+K_{t}^{1-\alpha}+(1-\delta)K_{t}\right)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the first order conditions are:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{C_{t}}-\lambda_{t} & = & 0\\
-\lambda_{t}+\beta\lambda_{t+1}\left((1-\alpha)K_{t+1}^{-\alpha}+1-\delta\right) & = & 0\\
{\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\ \beta^{t}\lambda_{t}K_{t} & \leq & 0\ \mbox{(Transversality Condition)}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Simplifying the conditions above, we get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
1 & = & \beta\frac{C_{t}}{C_{t+1}}\left[(1-\alpha)K_{t+1}^{-\alpha}+1-\delta\right]\ \mbox{(Euler Equation; Intertemporal Condition)}\\
K_{t+1}-K_{t} & = & K_{t}^{1-\alpha}-C_{t}-\delta K_{t}\ \mbox{(Resource Constraint)}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\left[(1-\alpha)K_{t+1}^{-\alpha}+1-\delta\right]$
\end_inset

 can be thought of as the gross real interest rate, and it is a function
 of how much capital is available in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

.
 More captial means that the interest rate is low because of the production
 function.
 If interest rate is high, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{C_{t}}{C_{t+1}}\leq1\iff C_{t+1}\geq C_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 So, a higher interst rate leads to a higher consumption growth rate.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta C_{t+1}\equiv C_{t+1}-C_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta K_{t+1}\equiv K_{t+1}-K_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What we want to find the optimal choice 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 as a function of state 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The household wants to choose an initial point 
\begin_inset Formula $(K_{0},C_{0})$
\end_inset

 on the stable arm.
 We have two variables 
\begin_inset Formula $(C_{t},K_{t})$
\end_inset

 and two equations, the combined Euler Equation and resource constraint.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the resource constratint, we obtain the transition rule for capital:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
K_{t}^{1-\alpha}+(1-\delta)K_{t} & = & C_{t}+K_{t+1}\nonumber \\
K_{t}^{1-\alpha}+K_{t}-\delta K_{t} & = & C_{t}+K_{t+1}\nonumber \\
K_{t}^{1-\alpha}-\delta K_{t}-C_{t} & = & K_{t+1}-K_{t}\equiv\triangle K_{t+1}\nonumber \\
\triangle K_{t+1} & = & K_{t}^{1-\alpha}-\delta K_{t}-C_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta K_{t+1}=0\iff C_{t}=K_{t}^{1-\alpha}-\delta K_{t}-C_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta K_{t+1}>0\iff C_{t}<K_{t}^{1-\alpha}-\delta K_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta K_{t+1}<0\iff C_{t}>K_{t}^{1-\alpha}-\delta K_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, at the steady state, 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{t+1}=K_{t}=K_{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t+1}=C_{t}=C_{*}$
\end_inset

.
 So, from the Euler equation, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1 & = & \beta\left[(1-\alpha)K_{*}^{-\alpha}+1-\delta\right]\\
K_{*} & = & \left[\frac{1}{\beta(1-\alpha)}+\frac{\delta-1}{1-\alpha}\right]^{-\frac{1}{\alpha}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note, at the steady stead 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta C_{t+1}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta K_{t+1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t+1}<C_{t}\iff K_{t+1}>K_{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t+1}>C_{t}\iff K_{t+1}<K_{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Log-Linearization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Instead of doing a global analysis of the phase diagram, we will focus on
 the local behavior at the intersection of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta C_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta K_{t+1}$
\end_inset

, which is the steady state point 
\begin_inset Formula $(K^{*},C^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 We will linearize the Euler Equation and the resource constraint by the
 first order Taylor expansion.
 (We disregard higher order terms because we are only concerned with local
 behavior).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Log-Linearlize the Euler Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F(C_{t},C_{t+1},K_{t+1})=\beta\frac{C_{t}}{C_{t+1}}\left[(1-\alpha)K_{t+1}^{-\alpha}+1-\delta\right]=1$
\end_inset

.
 The first-order Taylor approximation of 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 around 
\begin_inset Formula $(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
F(C_{t},C_{t+1},K_{t+1}) & \approx & F(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*})\\
 &  & +F_{1}(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*})(C_{t}-C^{*})\\
 &  & +F_{2}(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*})(C_{t+1}-C^{*})\\
 &  & +F_{3}(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*})(K_{t+1}-K^{*})\\
F(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*}) & = & 1\\
F_{1}(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*}) & = & \frac{1}{C^{*}}\\
F_{2}(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*}) & = & \left(-\frac{1}{C^{*}}\right)\\
F_{3}(C^{*},C^{*},K^{*}) & = & \beta(1-\alpha)(-\alpha)(K^{*})^{-\alpha-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Plugging these expression into the Taylor approximation we get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1 & \approx & +\frac{1}{C^{*}}(C_{t}-C^{*})-\frac{1}{C^{*}}(C_{t+1}-C^{*})-\beta(1-\alpha)\alpha(K^{*})^{-\alpha-1}(K_{t+1}-K^{*})\\
0 & \approx & \frac{C_{t}-C^{*}}{C^{*}}-\frac{C_{t+1}-C^{*}}{C^{*}}-\beta(1-\alpha)\alpha(K^{*})^{-\alpha}\frac{K_{t+1}-K^{*}}{K^{*}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\log(1+x)\approx x$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\approx0$
\end_inset

, then we can approximate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{C_{t}-C^{*}}{C^{*}}\approx\log\left(\frac{C_{t}}{C^{*}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 When apply this approximation method to the Taylor approximation, we get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0\approx\hat{c}_{t}-\hat{c}_{t+1}-\beta(1-\alpha)\alpha(K^{*})^{-\alpha}\hat{k}_{t+1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{t}=\log\left(\frac{C_{t}}{C^{*}}\right)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{t+1}=\log\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C^{*}}\right)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}_{t+1}=\log\left(\frac{K_{t+1}}{K^{*}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 This is the log-linearlized version of the Euler Equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Log-Linearize the Resource Constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $G(C_{t},K_{t},K_{t+1})=K_{t+1}+C_{t}-K_{t}^{1-\alpha}+(1-\delta)K_{t}=0$
\end_inset

.
 The first-order Taylor approximation of 
\begin_inset Formula $G(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 around 
\begin_inset Formula $(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
G(C_{t},K_{t},K_{t+1}) & \approx & G(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*})\\
 &  & +G_{1}(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*})(C_{t}-C^{*})\\
 &  & +G_{2}(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*})(K_{t}-K^{*})\\
 &  & +G_{3}(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*})(K_{t+1}-K^{*})\\
G(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*}) & = & 0\\
G_{1}(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*}) & = & 1\\
G_{2}(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*}) & = & -(1-\alpha)(K^{*})^{-\alpha}-(1-\delta)\\
G_{3}(C^{*},K^{*},K^{*}) & = & 1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Plugging these expression into the Taylor approximation, we get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
0 & \approx & (C_{t}-C^{*})-\left[(1-\alpha)(K^{*})^{-\alpha}-(1-\delta)\right](K_{t}-K^{*})+(K_{t+1}-K^{*})\nonumber \\
0 & \approx & C^{*}\left(\frac{C_{t}-C^{*}}{C^{*}}\right)-\left[(1-\alpha)(K^{*})^{-\alpha}-(1-\delta)\right]K^{*}\left(\frac{K_{t}-K^{*}}{K^{*}}\right)+K^{*}\left(\frac{K_{t}-K^{*}}{K^{*}}\right)\nonumber \\
0 & \approx & \left(\frac{C^{*}}{K^{*}}\right)\hat{c}_{t}-\left[(1-\alpha)(K^{*})^{-\alpha}-(1-\delta)\right]\hat{k}_{t}+\hat{k}_{t+1}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

This is the log-linearlized version of the capital accumulation rule.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Matrix Form
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We put the two log-linearlized equations into matrix form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
\alpha\left(1-\beta(1-\delta)\right) & 1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t+1}\\
\hat{c}_{t+1}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{\beta} & -\left[\frac{1}{1-\alpha}\left(\frac{1}{\beta}-1+\delta\right)-\delta\right]\\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t}\\
\hat{c}_{t}
\end{array}\right)\nonumber \\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t+1}\\
\hat{c}_{t+1}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
\alpha\left(1-\beta(1-\delta)\right) & 1
\end{array}\right)^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{\beta} & -\left[\frac{1}{1-\alpha}\left(\frac{1}{\beta}-1+\delta\right)-\delta\right]\\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t}\\
\hat{c}_{t}
\end{array}\right)\nonumber \\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t+1}\\
\hat{c}_{t+1}
\end{array}\right)=M\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t}\\
\hat{c}_{t}
\end{array}\right)
\end{gather}

\end_inset

The matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 summarizes the information of the Euler equation (first-order condition)
 and capital accumulation rule (resource constraint), and it tells us how
 the household travels from an initial 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{k}_{0},\hat{c}_{0})$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 only provides a necessary condition to find the optimal path.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Our next task is to deterimine the properties of 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 by obtaining its eigenvectors and eigenvalues.
 Consider the equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
M\psi=\mu\psi
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 is the eigenvector and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 is the eigenvalue.
 Such a 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 is found by solving 
\begin_inset Formula $\det(M-\mu I)=0$
\end_inset

, which is a quadratic when 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

.
 After solving the equation, we will find two roots, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 For now, we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{2}$
\end_inset

 are real roots and that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}\neq\mu_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote the eigenvector associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
M\psi_{1} & = & \mu_{1}\psi_{1}\\
M\psi_{2} & = & \mu_{2}\psi_{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We then put these two equations into matrix form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
M\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right] & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mu_{1} & 0\\
0 & \mu_{2}
\end{array}\right]\nonumber \\
M & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mu_{1} & 0\\
0 & \mu_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We plug this expressing for 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 into the matrix equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t+1}\\
\hat{c}_{t+1}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mu_{1} & 0\\
0 & \mu_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t}\\
\hat{c}_{t}
\end{array}\right)\nonumber \\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t+1}\\
\hat{c}_{t+1}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mu_{1} & 0\\
0 & \mu_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{t}\\
\hat{c}_{t}
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, we have established a new cooridnate system that uses 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{2}$
\end_inset

 as axes, while the origin represents the steady state.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\hat{k}_{0}^{A} & \hat{c}_{0}^{A}\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 be a vector where both components are less than their steady state values.
 Note, in the diagram, the vector of point 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{1}$
\end_inset

, so its second component in the new coordinate system will be zero.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}^{A}\\
\hat{c}^{A}
\end{array}\right) & = & M\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{0}^{A}\\
\hat{c}_{0}^{A}
\end{array}\right)\\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}^{A}\\
\hat{c}^{A}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mu_{1} & 0\\
0 & \mu_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{0}^{A}\\
\hat{c}_{0}^{A}
\end{array}\right)\\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}^{A}\\
\hat{c}^{A}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mu_{1} & 0\\
0 & \mu_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left(\begin{array}{c}
x\\
0
\end{array}\right)\\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}^{A}\\
\hat{c}^{A}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\mu_{1}x\\
0
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\hat{k}_{0}^{B} & \hat{c}_{0}^{B}\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 be a vector where both components are greater than their steady state values.
 Note, in the diagram, the vector of point 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{2}$
\end_inset

, so its first component in the new coordinate system will be zero.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}^{B}\\
\hat{c}^{B}
\end{array}\right) & = & M\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{0}^{B}\\
\hat{c}_{0}^{B}
\end{array}\right)\\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}^{B}\\
\hat{c}^{B}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mu_{1} & 0\\
0 & \mu_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}_{0}^{B}\\
\hat{c}_{0}^{B}
\end{array}\right)\\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}^{A}\\
\hat{c}^{A}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\mu_{1} & 0\\
0 & \mu_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
x
\end{array}\right)\\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
\hat{k}^{A}\\
\hat{c}^{A}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\mu_{2}x
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 has one eigenvalue that is less than one in absolute value and one that
 is greater than 1 in absolute value.
 Then, given an initial 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}_{0}$
\end_inset

, we want to choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{0}$
\end_inset

 on the line formed by the eigenvector with the corresponding eigenvalue
 that is less than one in absolute value because this is the stable arm.
 As a result, that the path converges to the origin, i.e., the steady state.
 Below is an outline of the different cases concerning the eigenvalues
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mu_{1}|>1>|\mu_{2}|$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the stable arm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mu_{2}|>1>|\mu_{1}|$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the stable arm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mu_{1}|>|\mu_{2}|>1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mu_{2}|>|\mu_{1}|>1$
\end_inset

, then there is no converegence to the steady state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $1>|\mu_{1}|>|\mu_{2}|$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $1>|\mu_{2}|>|\mu_{1}|$
\end_inset

, then we are close to global convergence, so there are infinitely many
 solutions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}_{t}=4\%$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{t}=3\%$
\end_inset

, we can rewrite the coordinates of this vector in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 using eigenvectors
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
4\%\\
3\%
\end{array}\right) & = & 0.8\psi_{1}+1.1\psi_{2}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]\left(\begin{array}{c}
0.8\\
1.1
\end{array}\right)\\
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{c}
4\%\\
3\%
\end{array}\right) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
0.8\\
1.1
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Multiplying the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\hat{k}_{t} & \hat{c}_{t}\end{array}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 on the left by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\psi_{1} & \psi_{2}\end{array}\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

 gives a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

new address
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to the point using 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{2}$
\end_inset

 as axes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Simplified Stochastic Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We will now add stochastic shocks to the simplified RBC model.
 The constrained maximization problem is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\log C_{t}\right]\\
\mbox{s.t.} & K_{t+1}-K_{t}=A_{t}K_{t}^{1-\alpha}-C_{t}-\delta K_{t}\\
 & \log A_{t}=\rho\log A_{t-1}+\epsilon_{t}\\
 & \epsilon_{t}\sim i.i.d.N(0,\sigma^{2})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In order to solve the model, we want to find the policy function 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}=f(K_{t},A_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 To do this, we log-linearize the Euler equation (intertemporal condition)
 and the two constraints:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
0 & = & E_{t}\left[\hat{c}_{t}-\hat{c}_{t+1}+\left(1-\beta(1-\delta)\right)\left(\hat{a}_{t+1}-\alpha\hat{k}_{t+1}\right)\right]\\
0 & = & -\left[\frac{1}{1-\alpha}\left(\frac{1}{\beta}-1+\delta\right)-\delta\right]\hat{c}_{t}+\frac{1}{1-\alpha}\left(\frac{1}{\beta}-1+\delta\right)\hat{a}_{t}-\hat{k}_{t+1}+\frac{1}{\beta}\hat{k}_{t}\\
\hat{a}_{t} & = & \rho\hat{a}_{t-1}+\epsilon_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

In this system, we have 2 state variables 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{a}_{t},\hat{k}_{t})$
\end_inset

 and 1 control variable 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{c}_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can rewrite this sytem of 3 variables and 3 equations in matrix form
 by finding a matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 that characterizes the three equations above.
 Remember, 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 only provides a necessary condition to find the optimal path.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 has two eigenvalues that are less than one in absolute value and one eigenvalue
 that is greater than 1 in absolute value (i.e., it has 2 stable roots and
 1 unstable root), then we want to choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{0}$
\end_inset

 on the plane formed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 As a result, the path converges to the origin, i.e., the steady state.
\end_layout

